Phase 8.4 - Promote stored-volume rendering and adapt temporal sampling
  Depends on: Phase 8.3.
  Outcome: stored-volume rendering is the default volumetric implementation, with existing temporal
    machinery adapted to amortize its view traces; retained density/lighting remain independently owned.
  [ ] Reconcile PBR.CloudVolume.Proposal.md / Status and intent, Ownership and integration and Initial
      controls with the authorized default promotion and subsequent removal. Update selection/default
      settings, ConfigLib labels and runtime construction so normal Volumetric mode uses stored-volume
      rendering without selecting experimental option2. Preserve native Off/Simple semantics and
      Full/Half/Quarter controls. Use a clean settings break, no migration or silent error fallback to
      either retiring implementation. Keep their temporary selection only until removal in8.5.
  [ ] Reconcile the newly authorized temporal view design with PBR.CloudVolume.Proposal.md / Ownership
      and integration, Rendering the published volume and Initial controls before implementation.
      Supersede the no-temporal-view restriction explicitly; keep stored-volume resource ownership
      and exact constant-cell transport. Update PBR.Clouds.md only for behavior actually delivered.
  [ ] Inspect canonical CloudVolumeReconstruction, CloudTemporalHistory, blue-noise resources and
      temporal/resolve shaders and their payload contracts. Identify reusable camera/material-motion,
      history admission, sequence, filtering and lifecycle machinery. Extract narrowly shared owners
      or helpers where compatible; adapt existing temporal ownership and payload boundaries instead of
      copying a second temporal stack or importing retiring transport, hierarchy or shadow-cache owners.
      Put retained temporal code in its steady-state cloud domain; identify every dependency that8.5
      must preserve. Do not build a permanent multi-renderer framework for the temporary coexistence.
  [ ] Specify the reduced fresh-trace schedule, temporal sample sequence, history length/weights and
      disocclusion fallback. Use the existing blue-noise resource/sequence where possible. Distinguish
      spatial/subpixel sampling from stochastic longitudinal sampling: blue noise alone saves no work.
      Prefer amortizing pixel/subpixel traces while retaining exact intervals within each traced ray;
      any occupied-cell subsampling requires an explicit revised estimator/transport contract first.
      Preserve shared Full/Half/Quarter controls and a compiled full-resolution reference bypass.
  [ ] Define a compatible physical history payload and required depth/completion/confidence metadata
      for compact radiance, opacity and all three bucket responses. Account for current depth clipping,
      liquid/particle occlusion, source encoding and final six-attachment OIT composition in both PBR
      modes. Reuse hardware filtering; do not average unrelated encoded values or failed ray prefixes.
  [ ] Reuse correct camera reprojection and native wind/material motion exactly once. Retain history
      across ordinary camera motion, day/night, fog, exposure and weather-light changes; use responsive
      weighting for changing illumination rather than resetting every frame. Reject incompatible
      world/layout/material identities and genuinely disoccluded samples. Distinguish partial density/
      lighting publication from incompatible replacement so normal bounded updates do not clear all
      history. Define local rejection/reactivity for changed geometry and stale-to-refreshed lighting.
  [ ] Implement shared history capture/commit and blue-noise scheduling through existing typed GPU
      programs, bindings and submission abstractions. Commit only after successful complete submission;
      handle first frame, missing inputs, failure, resize, selection, reload and teardown. Keep volume
      solver fences/publication independent, no CPU waits or duplicate global UBOs. User-configured
      path disablement selects compile-time options. Charge history targets and bandwidth separately
      from volume storage, reuse allocations and justify every additional buffer/attachment.
  [ ] Extend only affected existing fixtures for default selection, sequence progression, reprojection,
      identity/encoding, valid commit, disocclusion and lifecycle contracts. Run focused cached incremental
      builds/tests through subagents; reuse unchanged receipts. No approximation-quality, noise,
      convergence or image-error assertions, broad baseline matrix or routine packaging.
  [ ] Compare matched current view against temporal view at unchanged volume/settings/resolution:
      fresh rays and cell visits, trace/temporal/reconstruction and total GPU cost, CPU submission,
      history memory/bandwidth and retained/rejected coverage. Include stationary and moving cameras,
      wind, evolving weather/light, depth edges and replacement. Reuse capture evidence where relevant;
      do not assume fewer traces outweigh history overhead or conflate resolution changes with savings.
  [ ] Obtain user-run review for stationary noise, edges, ghosting, motion/disocclusion and changing
      lighting. Record measured tradeoffs and unresolved limits, reconcile proposal/current docs and
      independently review default ownership, preserved temporal contracts and retirement boundaries.
      Never launch
      the game or infer appearance/FPS acceptance from fixtures; final packaging stays in Phase 9.
  [ ] Completion: stored-volume rendering is the default; temporal sampling reduces fresh integration work,
      compatible history is
      retained safely, reusable machinery is shared with minimal duplication, and total costs plus
      user-reviewed appearance are explicit. No silent weakening of stored-density/transport contracts.

